{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":443585,"items":[{"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) encounter challenges such as hallucination and factual errors in knowledge-intensive tasks. One the one hand, LLMs sometimes struggle to generate reliable answers based on the black-box parametric knowledge, due to the lack of responsible knowledge. Moreover, fragmented knowledge facts extracted by knowledge retrievers fail to provide explicit and coherent reasoning paths for improving LLM reasoning. To address these challenges, we propose KG-CoT, a novel knowledge-augmented paradigm that leverages a small-scale step-by-step graph reasoning model to reason over knowledge graphs (KGs) and utilizes a reasoning path generation method to generate chains of reasoning with high confidence for large-scale LLMs. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our KG-CoT significantly improves the performance of LLMs on knowledge-intensive question answering tasks, such as  multi-hop, single-hop, and open-domain question answering benchmarks, without fine-tuning LLMs. KG-CoT outperforms the CoT prompting as well as prior retrieval-augmented and knowledge base question answering baselines. Moreover, KG-CoT can reduce the number of API calls and cost and generalize to various LLM backbones in a lightweight plug-and-play manner.</jats:p>","DOI":"10.24963/ijcai.2024/734","type":"proceedings-article","is-referenced-by-count":12,"title":["KG-CoT: Chain-of-Thought Prompting of Large Language Models over Knowledge Graphs for Knowledge-Aware Question Answering"],"author":[{"given":"Ruilin","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Natural Language Processing and Knowledge Graph Lab, School of Computer Science and Technology, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Feng","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Natural Language Processing and Knowledge Graph Lab, School of Computer Science and Technology, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Long","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Natural Language Processing and Knowledge Graph Lab, School of Computer Science and Technology, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xianzhi","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Data Science and Machine Intelligence Lab, University of Technology Sydney, Sydney, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guandong","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Data Science and Machine Intelligence Lab, University of Technology Sydney, Sydney, Australia"},{"name":"The Education University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Proceedings of the Thirty-ThirdInternational Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8]]},"URL":"https://doi.org/10.24963/ijcai.2024/734"},{"DOI":"10.1109/iscait69154.2026.11477466","title":["Evidence-Driven Chain-of-Thought Prompting with Multi-Dimensional Evidence Evaluation for Medical Question Answering"],"author":[{"given":"Zezhou","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software Technology, Dalian University of Technology,Dalian,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,23]]},"container-title":["2026 5th International Symposium on Computer Applications and Information Technology (ISCAIT)"],"is-referenced-by-count":0,"URL":"https://doi.org/10.1109/iscait69154.2026.11477466","type":"proceedings-article"},{"DOI":"10.5626/jok.2025.52.1.29","title":["Chain-of-Thought and Chain-of-Verification Prompting for Grammar-based Test Case Generation"],"author":[{"given":"A","family":"diti","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sang-Ki","family":"Ko","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,31]]},"container-title":["Journal of KIISE"],"is-referenced-by-count":1,"URL":"https://doi.org/10.5626/jok.2025.52.1.29","type":"journal-article"},{"DOI":"10.1109/cbase67452.2025.11335438","title":["Positive-Negative Chain-of-Thought Prompting for Table and Text Financial Question Answering"],"author":[{"given":"Xingyi","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Chongqing Jiaotong University,School of Information Science and Engineering,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ren","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chongqing Jiaotong University,School of Information Science and Engineering,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianxi","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chongqing Jiaotong University,School of Information Science and Engineering,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiao","family":"Xiao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chongqing Jiaotong University,School of Information Science and Engineering,Chongqing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,24]]},"container-title":["2025 4th International Conference on Cloud Computing, Big Data Application and Software Engineering (CBASE)"],"is-referenced-by-count":1,"URL":"https://doi.org/10.1109/cbase67452.2025.11335438","type":"proceedings-article"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Medical Visual Question Answering (MedVQA) lies at the intersection of computer vision, natural language processing, and clinical decision-making, aiming to generate accurate responses from medical images paired with complex inquiries. Despite recent advances in vision–language models (VLMs), their use in healthcare remains limited by a lack of interpretability and a tendency to produce direct, unexplainable outputs. This opacity undermines their reliability in medical settings, where transparency and justification are critically important. To address this limitation, we propose a zero-shot chain-of-thought prompting framework that guides VLMs to perform multi-step reasoning before arriving at an answer. By encouraging the model to break down the problem, analyze both visual and contextual cues, and construct a stepwise explanation, the approach makes the reasoning process explicit and clinically meaningful. We evaluate the framework on the PMC-VQA benchmark, which includes authentic radiological images and expert-level prompts. In a comparative analysis of three leading VLMs, Gemini 2.5 Pro achieved the highest accuracy (72.48%), followed by Claude 3.5 Sonnet (69.00%) and GPT-4o Mini (67.33%). The results demonstrate that chain-of-thought prompting significantly improves both reasoning transparency and performance in MedVQA tasks.</jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390/math13142322","type":"journal-article","is-referenced-by-count":7,"title":["Analyzing Diagnostic Reasoning of Vision–Language Models via Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought Prompting in Medical Visual Question Answering"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0009-0005-1684-8211","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fatema Tuj Johora","family":"Faria","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Ahsanullah University of Science and Technology, Dhaka 1208, Bangladesh"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0029-0859","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Laith H.","family":"Baniata","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Gachon University, Seongnam 13120, Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7676-9869","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ahyoung","family":"Choi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Gachon University, Seongnam 13120, Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0281-1726","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sangwoo","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computing, Gachon University, Seongnam 13120, Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"container-title":["Mathematics"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,21]]},"URL":"https://doi.org/10.3390/math13142322"},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract</title>
                <p>Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting has become a standard technique for eliciting multi-step reasoning from large language models (LLMs), and several refinements — zero-shot CoT, few-shot CoT, self-consistency decoding, and tree-of-thought (ToT) search — have each been reported to improve reasoning accuracy on frontier, closed-weight models. Comparatively little controlled evidence exists on how these strategies perform, relative to one another, on the small (3.8–8B parameter) open-weight instruction-tuned models that practitioners increasingly deploy for cost and privacy reasons. This paper reports a controlled empirical comparison of four CoT-based prompting strategies (zero-shot CoT, few-shot CoT, self-consistency, and tree-of-thought) across three open-weight instruction-tuned models — Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct, and Llama-3-8B-Instruct — on a 100-item subset of the GSM8K grade-school arithmetic benchmark. We report per-condition accuracy with 95% confidence intervals and test pairwise differences using McNemar's exact test on paired, per-item outcomes. Across all three models, few-shot CoT and self-consistency significantly outperform zero-shot CoT (accuracy gains of 12–40 percentage points, p &lt; 0.05 in every model), while self-consistency with three sampled reasoning paths (k = 3) offers no statistically significant improvement over single-sample few-shot CoT. Tree-of-thought scores below few-shot CoT in every model (significantly so in two of three) and is statistically indistinguishable from the much cheaper zero-shot baseline in all three, despite requiring multiple sampled branches per answer. These results suggest that, for short, single-path arithmetic reasoning at this model scale, the presence of worked exemplars is the dominant driver of accuracy gains, while search-based deliberation (ToT) and low-sample self-consistency add inference cost without a corresponding, statistically reliable accuracy benefit.</p>","DOI":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-11327774/v1","type":"posted-content","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Does More Deliberation Help? A Comparative Evaluation of Chain-of-Thought Prompting Strategies for Grade-School Math Reasoning in Open-Source Instruction-Tuned Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https://orcid.org/0009-0005-4775-1068","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Arun","family":"Kumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Kharagpur"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]},"URL":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11327774/v1"},{"DOI":"10.1109/fllm63129.2024.10852507","title":["Can Separators Improve Chain-of-Thought Prompting?"],"author":[{"given":"Yoonjeong","family":"Park","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Yonsei University,Seoul,Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hyunjin","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Yonsei University,Seoul,Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chanyeol","family":"Choi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Linq,Cambridge,Massachusetts,United States"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junseong","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Linq,Cambridge,Massachusetts,United States"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jy-Yong","family":"Sohn","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Yonsei University,Seoul,Republic of Korea"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,26]]},"container-title":["2024 2nd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"],"is-referenced-by-count":2,"URL":"https://doi.org/10.1109/fllm63129.2024.10852507","type":"proceedings-article"},{"DOI":"10.2139/ssrn.5285532","title":["Prompting Science Report 2: The Decreasing Value of Chain of Thought in Prompting"],"author":[{"given":"Lennart","family":"Meincke","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ethan R.","family":"Mollick","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lilach","family":"Mollick","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dan","family":"Shapiro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"is-referenced-by-count":12,"URL":"https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.5285532","type":"posted-content"},{"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;
 We study a specific but important failure mode in financial LLMs: numerical&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 errors in task-specifically fine-tuned small models are statistically&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 structured rather than random (chi-squared = 41.97, p &amp;lt; 0.001), and this&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 structure makes deterministic post-hoc correction more effective than&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 reasoning augmentation in our controlled experiments. Scaling reasoning via&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) —&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 the dominant paradigms for improving LLM numerical accuracy — both degrade&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 performance in this setting (CoT: -9.0pp; RAG: -7.5pp; p &amp;lt; 0.001,&amp;nbsp;
